== Proofs from Scripture

1\. For #emph[Scripture proofs,] there are very many in both the Old and the New Testament. What the apostle says of #emph[godliness] in 1 Tim 4:8, that it “is profitable for all things, holding promise for the present life and for the one to come,” I may say of #emph[charity:] it is profitable for all things, holding promises of #emph[temporal blessings] as well as of #emph[spiritual] and #emph[eternal] ones.

Deut 15:10: “Give generously to your brother, and do not let your heart be grieved when you do so. And because of this the #smallcaps[Lord] your God will bless you in all your work and in everything to which you put your hand.” It is not your own labor or care but the divine blessing that makes rich, and see, here is a #emph[Scripture deed] that settles that blessing on the generous. We have the like in Prov 3:9, 10: “Honor the #smallcaps[Lord] with your wealth,” by giving out a portion of it for the relief of the poor, “then your barns will be filled with plenty.” And as your #emph[barns,] so your #emph[shop,] your #emph[cellars] and #emph[warehouses,] where you store your goods, will be filled with abundance: “and your vats will burst with new wine.” This seems to be a hyperbole, yet it means no less than this: #emph[that God’s usual way is to make good returns of all we lay out on him and his;] and that our wisest way, both to secure and to increase what we have, is to make ourselves creditors to his needy saints.

A compassionate heart and a helping hand will gather by spending. Such #emph[giving] is #emph[getting;] such bounty is the shortest way to plenty. So the wise man adds, in Prov 11:24: “One scatters, yet increases all the more; another withholds more than is right, only to become poor.” Who is it here who scatters? Not the man who wastes his estate on his throat, his back and his belly, or, with the #emph[prodigal,] on #emph[harlots;] but the man who “casts his bread upon the waters,” as the expression is in Eccl 11:1; the man who “has scattered abroad his gifts to the poor,” as in Ps 112:9. This is the scatterer in that text. And what of him? Is he wasted? Is he impoverished? See, the very opposite: the man has grown rich; #emph[he is increased by scattering.] Let the man who withholds more than is right look to it: he is the more likely of the two to be found shortly on the dunghill. If you would secure yourself from beggary, do not keep God’s beggars from your door. Do not send away empty those whose needs, though not their tongues, cry in your ears, #emph[Give for the Lord’s sake.] Do not fear to lose by laying out. Your #emph[laying up] what you should #emph[lay out] has the most danger in it. The divine curse may wither in your hand what you hold fast against the divine command.

In the following verse we read further, “The generous soul will be made fat.” The #emph[soul] is often taken in Scripture to mean the #emph[man,] and so it is here: it is the same as the #emph[generous man. To be made fat] means #emph[to prosper,] to be #emph[full] and #emph[abound,] or #emph[to grow rich in this world’s goods.] And so it will be, if this Scripture may be believed.

#emph[The generous soul:] in the #emph[Hebrew,] as you#super[+] may find it noted in the margin, it is #emph[the soul of blessing.] The merciful man is a blessing, and has a blessing for those who need him; and he who is a blessing will be blessed. The Lord God will bless him with an increase of his wealth, the man who has most emptied himself for God and his people. It is true that the soul of the generous man, taken in the proper sense, will be a gainer by his liberality. No Christians are more #emph[thriving] than the #emph[merciful Christians;] what they spend in temporal things is abundantly returned in spiritual things. But there is so much of the #emph[carnal] even in too many Christians, and we need encouragements suited to our natures. So these outward encouragements are added to our spiritual advantages (which are the greatest), since they often take hold most on our too carnal hearts. So this is the sense of the place: the generous man, as the reward and encouragement of his liberality, will prosper in the world. And as it follows, #emph[he who waters] the soul of the needy, his waters will not fail, but he #emph[will himself be watered also.] God will give back to him according as he has given out to others.

And for their further encouragement the wise man says, in Prov 19:17: “Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the #smallcaps[Lord], and He will repay him for what he has given.” If what you give is repaid to you again, how can it be said to be lost? Indeed the poor to whom you give are not able to repay you. Yet you have so all-sufficient a surety as God himself, who has undertaken the repayment, so you need not doubt of it. Oh, happy is the man who becomes a creditor to his Creator and makes God his debtor! The earth is God’s, and all its fullness. Heaven and earth will be emptied before such a man goes without a royal payment.

Do not fear to be a usurer, so long as you lend to God. Make him your debtor, and he will surely pay you principal and interest. Allow him his time (for that he will have), and he will not only pay the debt, but allow you something for your waiting. The ungodly man borrows and does not pay back; but this will never be said of the righteous God. He is just, and will make good payment of whatever is lent to him.

It is true that the Lord loves to deal #emph[on trust,] and is accustomed to make payment by way of #emph[returns.] He will be trusted; and those who will not give him credit, let them put their estates into surer hands, if they can. And he is accustomed to pay by way of #emph[returns:] it may be neither in the same kind nor in the same country. Sometimes he does not pay in the same kind, bread for bread, or clothes for clothes, or money for money. Nor perhaps will he make his payment in this foreign land where you are a merchant adventurer. He may give you his bill of exchange to receive it in your own country, that better country which is your home and inheritance, which will be the reward of your works and of the labor of love which you show to his name. And if he does this, does he do you any wrong? Is it not best for you to receive your goods at your home? Is it any harm to you to receive silver for your brass, gold for your silver, rubies for your gold, a treasure in heaven for your treasure on earth? Do not fear that he will fall behind in paying you. Yet do not balk, even if you should be put to wait for payment in full till hereafter. And for the present you may depend on this, from Prov 28:27: “Whoever gives to the poor will not lack.” He will have in hand what is needful at least. #emph[He will not lack:] and there may be more in this word than if he had said, I will pay you all at once. For all that you have, though you hold it ever so close, cannot secure you from ever coming to want. Great as your abundance is, you may come to want before you die. But what your abundance cannot do, this promise of God can do, and does, for you: it gives you security that you will never lack.

Again, Eccl 11:1: “Cast your bread upon the waters, for after many days you will find it again.” By #emph[bread] is meant here all things necessary for the support of men’s lives. And by #emph[casting] their bread upon the waters is meant their giving freely of what they have for the relief of the poor, whose watery eyes betray their great need. What you bestow on them may seem as clearly lost and thrown away as what is thrown down the river or cast into the sea, where there is no likelihood of receiving it again, because it is given to those who can in no way repay you. From this arose that #emph[Greek proverb,] εἰς ὕδωρ σπείρεις, #emph[you sow upon the water,] which is usually applied to those who bestow kindnesses where they are utterly lost.

Yet “after many days you will find it again.” #emph[Lavater, in locum] \[on this passage\], says that here is implied a promise of long life to the charitable man: that God will lengthen out his days to a good old age. Though this proves true in many, yet I do not think it is intended here, or is the meaning of the words. Rather the meaning is this: though your reward does not appear at once, yet if you bestow your alms with a sincere heart, it will not be lost, but will certainly be returned into your bosom with increase.

The Lord says yet further by the prophet in Isa 58:7, 8, etc.: “If you share your bread with the hungry, and bring the poor and homeless into your home, and clothe the naked when you see him, and do not hide yourself from your own flesh and blood,” by turning your face from him, “then” (see what a gracious promise of remarkable blessings follows on this) “your light will break forth like the dawn.” That is, then the night of your adversity will be driven away, and the dawn of your prosperity will break forth like the morning light. “Your righteousness will go before you”: that is, the fruit and reward of your charity will be visible to all. “The glory of the #smallcaps[Lord] will be your rear guard”: that is, the God of glory will by his power and providence both go before you and follow you with his blessing. And verse 10: “If you draw out your soul to the hungry and satisfy the afflicted soul,” that is, give him such hearty relief as will satisfy his need, “then your light will go forth in the darkness”: your adversity will be turned into prosperity. And “the #smallcaps[Lord] will always guide you; He will satisfy your soul in drought”: as you satisfied the poor man’s soul by supplying his needs, so the Lord will supply you with enough in the greatest dearth and famine. “And He will strengthen your frame. You will be like a well-watered garden, like a spring whose waters never fail”: an emblem of a flourishing and prosperous condition, which usually follows and accompanies merciful men.

Yes, our blessed Savior in Matt 19:29 promises that those who for his sake part with their estates, in this or any other way, or with any part or portion of them, #emph[will receive a hundredfold here, and inherit eternal life hereafter.] Here in this life a man will have the return of a hundredfold. Many Christians have found this true in their own experience, and with thankfulness to God have acknowledged as much.

And what a variety of expressions our Savior uses in Luke 6:38 to assure us of a large reward here for all our labor of love shown to his ministers and members! “Give,” he says, “and it will be given to you#super[+] again: a good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over.” For your#super[+] encouragement to this duty of giving to the poor, our Savior first adds a general promise of return: “Give, and it will be given to you#super[+] again.” In this he gives you#super[+] a #emph[bill in his own hand] that you#super[+] will be no loser by what you#super[+] give to his poor, but will be repaid, and that with advantage, as the following words declare: you#super[+] will have “a good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over.” We count it good measure when it is #emph[heaped up.] When it is not only heaped up but #emph[pressed down,] that is more. But when it is heaped up, pressed down, and then #emph[heaped up] and #emph[running over] again, who can help saying that this is good measure indeed? Yet this is how God deals with merciful men: they will have mercies and blessings #emph[heaped up, pressed down, and running over.] How truly, then, may I take up the words of the psalmist: “Blessed is the one who cares for the poor”! He will not only be blessed, but he will have blessings #emph[heaped up and running over!] The apostle St. #emph[Paul] expresses this very #emph[emphatically] by the comparison of #emph[reaping] and #emph[sowing] (2 Cor 9:6): “Whoever sows sparingly will also reap sparingly, and whoever sows bountifully will also reap bountifully.” As men sow, so will they reap: the more liberally they give to good uses, the more bountifully they will receive from God. They will find a plentiful harvest as the fruit of all their cost and pains. Dr. #emph[Hammond,] in his Practical Catechism, quoting these words of the apostle, “whoever sows bountifully will also reap bountifully,” adds this gloss: “By #emph[reaping bountifully,] I take it, is meant not only God’s abundant repayments of glory in another world, but even his payments of temporal plenty and blessings here to those who have been willing to make that Christian use of the earthly talent committed to their stewardship.”

So you#super[+] see that there are so many promises, in both the Old and the New Testament, of #emph[temporal] as well as of spiritual and eternal blessings to the merciful, that no question can be made of the truth of the doctrine. And to use the expression of the #emph[doctor] mentioned before: “By all these testimonies from the word of God, in both the New and the Old Testament, I take this doctrine to be as clear as any in the Scripture: that the promise of temporal plenty to the generous is so distinct and infallible that it can be no less than

+ a very gross ignorance of plain Scripture not to observe it, and
+ an act of downright unbelief not to believe it.”
